==================================================
Lab 6: Server-Side Template Injection in a Sandboxed Environment
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website lets a content manager edit the template that generates each product description.
The site uses the Freemarker template engine with a sandbox, which is meant to limit what templates can do.
The sandbox is poorly implemented, so a template can still reach Java methods that read files on the server.

Goal: read /home/carlos/my_password.txt and submit its contents.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

A content manager logs in, opens the template editor for a product, and saves new template text.
The product page renders that template, filling in values such as the product's name and price.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The sandbox blocks some direct actions, but it does not block chained method calls on the objects it exposes.
Starting from the product object, a chain of Java method calls can reach a class that reads any file the server can access.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Object methods: Every Java object inherits methods from the Object class, such as getClass(), so ${object.getClass()} is reachable from any object the template can see.
Method chaining: Each call returns a new object, and the next call works on that object. The chain walks from the product class to a class whose location lets us resolve a path to a file.
Byte output: The file is read as bytes. ?join(" ") turns those bytes into space-separated decimal code points, which the page then shows.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

We save this as the template:

${product.getClass().getProtectionDomain().getCodeSource().getLocation().toURI().resolve("/home/carlos/my_password.txt").toURL().openStream().readAllBytes()?join(" ")}

The chain resolves the path to my_password.txt, opens it as a stream, and reads all of its bytes.
The page then shows those bytes as decimal numbers, such as 112 97 115 115, which convert to ASCII text.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Log in as content-manager and open a product's template editor.
Replace the template with the payload above and click Save template.
Open the product page and copy the decimal numbers shown.
Convert each number to its ASCII character, then paste the resulting string into "Submit solution".

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It logs in as content-manager and checks that the login worked.
* It reads the CSRF token from the template editor, then saves the payload with the fields the browser sends: csrf, template, and template-action=save.
* It loads the product page and searches for a run of space-separated numbers, which is the file's byte output.
* It converts each number to a character and prints the decoded text, so you can check it before submitting.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The sandbox filters what the template can call directly, but it does not stop a chain of allowed method calls from reaching sensitive classes.
Once the template can run any Java method on an exposed object, it can read files the web server process can access.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not let users edit template source. Let them change only the data that a fixed template displays.
If templates must be editable, use a sandbox that blocks reflection and dangerous classes, and do not rely on blocklists of method names.
Run the application with the lowest permissions it needs, so the process cannot read files like the password file.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
A sandbox that blocks single calls can still be bypassed by chaining allowed calls.
Read the decoded output carefully, since the number sequence may include other values from the page.
Never let users control template source.
